ratorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6a58962-cb06-519a-8c64-0dfa3b9c14d7" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aff4cbe-6fe4-5b7e-818d-a180abe9eb18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269488} {:answer/id #uuid "1ebb1f5f-41ff-5401-ad84-cc41307a79d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269527} {:answer/id #uuid "38362095-3b1c-5bfd-9cc4-30473fe3b5bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269776} {:answer/id #uuid "4003a8ec-c9e7-5741-ba8f-90325c814912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269851} {:answer/id #uuid "ea19c52e-9497-565a-b200-4435179ca96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4003a8ec-c9e7-5741-ba8f-90325c814912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269851} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcc5eb38-7a7e-586b-9f31-c5ca7c948e10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c98a2195-4ec8-5e2a-a4f5-76fb1079c255" :question/math-academy-id "q-141374" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{150}^{∘})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={150}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{150}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{150}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059665 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b931fa1-ee52-5db3-bac9-497efc06f498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269370} {:answer/id #uuid "2342b124-c9e0-58c0-a580-8d4f8695808a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269577} {:answer/id #uuid "69b15b7b-0cac-5374-93f1-818c8990768a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270207} {:answer/id #uuid "a358caaa-5c57-5746-b54c-5548bfb79b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270725} {:answer/id #uuid "dff6ea96-0ee2-5bc4-80b8-7b9f1617a512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b931fa1-ee52-5db3-bac9-497efc06f498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269370} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f2bd73b-78d1-5e9e-92ad-582b63f4ea0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "308956a9-f5f0-59d1-8748-67ba18adb1f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-141381" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059665 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a22d57d-9107-5ef5-9e1a-664d1e481595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269651} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7bf421-a5a6-51a7-bbde-dcaec6ed4724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269688} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0c5476-651b-5c29-aaf7-9c8b4495a59a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270462} {:answer/id #uuid "8a8eb468-d843-5e6f-a576-53b1846a95f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270466} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8ff2c0-77a5-54e0-af66-4eb8ca206ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a22d57d-9107-5ef5-9e1a-664d1e481595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269651} :answer-field/id #uuid "64d9c7cb-8c90-5531-a052-3b459d3163b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bedf2d85-2b35-59e9-bfbf-70576d25817c" :question/math-academy-id "q-141386" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall  the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "311f9529-efb3-578e-8744-809e1101f04a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269712} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3730b5-4247-558a-bff6-c3d95ce3ec3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270236} {:answer/id #uuid "83f3968d-ef3f-53f0-ba78-c6e9d8fb524c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270408} {:answer/id #uuid "86ca815b-5e6f-55e6-91c6-bd46a039a85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270438} {:answer/id #uuid "b1319d66-f133-598f-a450-27c576b92f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1319d66-f133-598f-a450-27c576b92f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270851} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca9f1ac-2135-555e-8f76-360ce0db72a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aeb1ad5f-0910-5dea-b64a-e03889cc2cc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-14145" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=10{x}^{6}-52{x}^{2}+50x-8,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4,$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $10,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 5,$ and $\\pm 10.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{8}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 8, & \\pm 4, & \\pm \\frac{8}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{10},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{5},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{8}{5},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $20$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 10=20.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f10e4a34-a512-5ff4-abbc-66e6830d63d0" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "110ee8cd-e830-5bb3-bab0-f16cb26fe2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269421} {:answer/id #uuid "13e31713-81cb-5dec-8652-93d586dd34fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269445} {:answer/id #uuid "29d7d2be-f02c-521d-b752-bf360c856fdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269648} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb20cc7-de16-5bcf-8e1b-43826770ca5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-6{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270109} {:answer/id #uuid "f84d763b-739b-535d-8995-04b2349046b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+6{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f84d763b-739b-535d-8995-04b2349046b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+6{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271448} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee48d03a-569e-51f8-be78-887782d15f39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48f5b61f-2f3f-5cda-a551-c1e861cdd220" :question/math-academy-id "q-141513" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $({y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2})-3$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2ay+{a}^{2}=(y+a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 6y+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ (y+6{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2})-3 & = \\\\ (y+6{)}^{2}-3 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec6076ff-1538-5bc0-973d-33d908b0b4d2" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31012e79-2696-578b-952b-a385f9bb0eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269711} {:answer/id #uuid "80e62b24-0b58-586c-ab5e-10a92a99e5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270386} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8982a4-a551-5a56-96cc-144410267cb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270506} {:answer/id #uuid "94a242c2-81bc-53a1-99fc-de5cfa4e26aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270571} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa96b06-cbed-595d-86c7-fdb048fe292f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31012e79-2696-578b-952b-a385f9bb0eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269711} :answer-field/id #uuid "40dc7a0f-2584-5d4a-b845-07be3a13c2db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b17fd33d-fef8-5118-8c44-b94f68ba1d49" :question/math-academy-id "q-141527" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}+11y+25.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+25\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $25$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+25\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+25\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec6076ff-1538-5bc0-973d-33d908b0b4d2" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20d0ce94-71f7-556b-a0d7-7b5e20af96e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269547} {:answer/id #uuid "5c56dfc4-4aef-5331-872d-da2b2274bae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270094} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd458c1-82ff-5e70-925e-2f5ec028580c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270924} {:answer/id #uuid "ca96e8bc-27e6-5cce-ac58-a15424f3db5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{7}{4})}^{2}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271049} {:answer/id #uuid "e14d5b9e-029a-5589-8dcb-8d46977be99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{7}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20d0ce94-71f7-556b-a0d7-7b5e20af96e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269547} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b5ddc0f-2757-59e8-9e14-a04b8fd5611f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6ca9c1a-eab9-55fb-98fa-ee3277281e1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141536" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}+7y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}y\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}y+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "129c0ce9-31d0-501f-9253-202530581425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269432} {:answer/id #uuid "1e413b35-35e1-5ce6-a283-f4ff4b83d4f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+10{)}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269522} {:answer/id #uuid "395d3e65-bf9a-53cf-a730-73e30942c7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269789} {:answer/id #uuid "899c0f97-acdd-567e-ab3c-221326e9fa7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270456} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c19f26-9f49-5230-b707-0daae3af6b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "395d3e65-bf9a-53cf-a730-73e30942c7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269789} :answer-field/id #uuid "12b561ff-d7d5-56bf-bd70-881cdbd1aea3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7d3420b-3f28-5142-8868-756f1394ea88" :question/math-academy-id "q-141537" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${x}^{2}+10x+16.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 5x+16\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $16$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+16\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+16\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+5{)}^{2}-9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4bc4897-6558-52b5-bb24-657fa38a810c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=6,\\,q=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400932} {:answer/id #uuid "62f6fb47-ecea-56a8-9070-83fc55e7254f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400933} {:answer/id #uuid "3d43940b-e27a-5dff-9dca-bf6857baf92f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400934} {:answer/id #uuid "bdeaea36-9352-52ca-8483-6b276a77d472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=6,\\,q=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400935} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc66d56-1a8c-5626-b30a-e9774929570d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d43940b-e27a-5dff-9dca-bf6857baf92f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400934} :answer-field/id #uuid "95bbc964-13ea-55a0-9065-a7c49f46fb65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd1c73ef-6818-5b51-baee-cca065c2f0da" :question/math-academy-id "q-141539" :question/problem "If the expression ${x}^{2}+6x+14$ is written as $(x+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+14\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $14$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{3}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+3{)}^{2}-{3}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+3{)}^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=3$ and $q=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184170 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1481d4a2-64b4-590a-9dbb-c39d3364bb59" :knowledge-point/title "Expressions Containing a Negative Linea